 five while markets and most raters favor a flip. Mackenzie's fundraising edge keeps this short of Likely D.", "watch": "Any late Mackenzie surge with blue-collar or Latino voters in the Lehigh Valley, or new polling showing the race tightening, would move this back toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Ryan Mackenzie (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Ryan Mackenzie won this Lehigh Valley district by the narrowest margin in Pennsylvania two years ago, and Democrats are making a serious bid to take it back with Bob Brooks. The race is among the most expensive House contests of the cycle, with Brooks raising about $2.4 million and benefiting from roughly $2.5 million in outside support, while Mackenzie has raised about $4.4 million and holds a cash advantage. A Green Party candidate was kept off the ballot, removing a potential spoiler.\n\nPolls show Brooks narrowly ahead, with a September Franklin & Marshall survey giving him a five-point lead among registered voters. Most expert ratings shifted toward Democrats in late September, and prediction markets price Brooks between 83 and 89 percent to win. The race remains competitive given Mackenzie's incumbency and fundraising, but the weight of the evidence favors a Democratic flip.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7234, "margin": 3.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7148, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7673, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7925, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7174, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7271, "margin": 4.2}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7743, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7833, "margin": 4.2}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.8054, "margin": 5.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.794, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.7967, "margin": 4.5}]}